<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Midas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. late 8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Mita of Mushki, King Midas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Midas was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> in the late 8th century BCE, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordion</a>. Assyrian records call him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">Mita</a> of Mushki and show him as a rival and later a correspondent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>. He married a princess of Cyme and was said to be the first foreigner to send an offering to Delphi. Greek tradition says he killed himself when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> overran Phrygia, and made him the king with the golden touch.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/midas.jpg" alt="Midas" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Midas Painter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red-figure_stamnos_Silenos_Mida_440BC_Cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
